Case Study 8 — Bar Chart of Marks
Problem. Draw a bar chart of five subject marks. Each bar's height must be proportional to the mark, and
clicking a bar should print its value on the screen.
Solution — BarChart.java (25 lines)
// Case Study 8: a simple bar chart applet
import java.applet.Applet;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.*;
public class BarChart extends Applet {

Page 36 of 62

SCSVMV University | Department of Computer Science and Engineering | Java Programming Laboratory

int[] marks = { 78, 91, 45, 66, 88 };
String msg = "Click a bar";
public void init() {
addMouseListener(new MouseAdapter() {
public void mouseClicked(MouseEvent e) {
int i = (e.getX() - 20) / 50;
if (i >= 0 && i < 5) msg = "Subject " + (i + 1) + " = " + marks[i];
repaint();
}
});
}
public void paint(Graphics g) {
g.drawString(msg, 20, 20);
for (int i = 0; i < marks.length; i++) {
g.setColor(marks[i] >= 50 ? Color.GREEN : Color.RED);
g.fillRect(20 + i * 50, 150 - marks[i], 40, marks[i]);
}
}
}
Output
Subject 2 = 91
#
# # # #
# # # # #
# # # # # green bars = pass, red bar = fail
# # R # #
1 2 3 4 5
Result
Simple applets demonstrating the life cycle, graphics drawing and a clickable colour palette were written and
executed with appletviewer.
